\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{The retained marginal in orthonormal coordinates}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{Apply \ref{fdip-000E} in orthonormal
charts #{C_X:X\to\mathbb R^I}, #{C_Y:Y\to\mathbb R^{J_Y}}
with finite index sets. The joint chart
#{C_E=(C_X\oplus C_Y)J^{-1}} uses the addition isometry #{J}.
Write
##{\begin{aligned}
\widehat Q&=C_EQC_E^{-1},\qquad
\widehat\Sigma=C_EQ^{-1}C_E^{-1},\\
\widehat S&=C_XSC_X^{-1},\\
L&=C_Xp_XC_E^{-1}:\mathbb R^{I\sqcup J_Y}\to\mathbb R^I.
\end{aligned}}
Thus #{L(\xi,\eta)=\xi}; its transpose represents #{\iota_X}.
The intrinsic inverse-compression identity in \ref{fdip-000E} gives
##{L\widehat\Sigma L^T=C_XS^{-1}C_X^{-1}
                         =\widehat S^{-1}.}
In the ordered delta bases for counting fields on #{R\sqcup U},
this is exactly #{(Q^{-1})_{RR}=S^{-1}}. For nonorthonormal charts,
coordinate functionals instead require their own Riesz
representatives, as in the covariance formula of \ref{fdip-000B}.}

\p{The marginal and conditional formulas in
\citet{Appendix A.2, equations (A.5)-(A.6), p. 200}{rasmussen2006gaussian}
use joint covariance, here #{\widehat\Sigma}. The inverse-block
notation is set out in
\citet{Appendix A.3, equations (A.11)-(A.13), pp. 201-202}{rasmussen2006gaussian}.
Its retained covariance is #{\widehat S^{-1}}, whereas
#{\widehat S} is retained precision.}

\p{For a finite index set #{K}, write
#{U_K=\operatorname{toLp}_2:\mathbb R^K\to
\operatorname{EuclideanSpace}(\mathbb R,K)} for the coordinate
wrapper, whose inverse is #{\operatorname{ofLp}}. The provider's
joint and retained charts are #{U_{I\sqcup J_Y}C_E} and
#{U_IC_X}; the displayed arrays act on their coordinate functions.}
\p{The density and volume comparison in \ref{fdip-00JU} applies to these
charts: #{\widehat\Sigma>0},
#{\widehat\Sigma^{-1}=\widehat Q}, and
#{\det\widehat\Sigma=(\det Q)^{-1}}. With
#{n=\dim E}, the centered provider density relative to coordinate
Lebesgue volume is
##{\begin{aligned}
&\frac{\exp(-\frac12z^T\widehat\Sigma^{-1}z)}
 {(2\pi)^{n/2}\sqrt{\det\widehat\Sigma}}\\
&\qquad=Z_Q^{-1}w_Q(C_E^{-1}z).
\end{aligned}}
Transporting the provider law by #{U_{I\sqcup J_Y}^{-1}}
therefore gives #{(C_E)_*\mu_Q}. Likewise its retained Gaussian
with covariance #{\widehat S^{-1}}, transported by #{U_I^{-1}},
is #{(C_X)_*\mu_S}.
The provider density theorem
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.Probability.multivariateGaussian_eq_withDensity}
requires strictly positive-definite covariance on a finite decidable
real coordinate type; its nonnegative density is converted by
#{\operatorname{ENNReal.ofReal}} for the measure constructor.}
\p{The [orthonormal chart](fdip-002M) and
\leanref{OrthonormalBasis.measurePreserving_repr} use the canonical
volumes on finite-dimensional real inner-product Borel spaces.
The latter chart takes values in the Euclidean #{\ell^2} wrapper.
The maps in \leanref{PiLp.volume_preserving_ofLp} and
\leanref{PiLp.volume_preserving_toLp} pass between that wrapper
and ordinary coordinate functions with their product volume.
The orthonormal representation followed by #{\operatorname{ofLp}}
is the volume-preserving coordinate chart in the density identity. The rectangular map #{L} is a different
map and need not preserve these volumes.}

\p{Mathlib's
\leanref{ProbabilityTheory.measurePreserving_restrict₂_multivariateGaussian}
applies to finite subsets #{J_0\subseteq I_0} of a decidable
coordinate type, an arbitrary mean and a positive-semidefinite
covariance. Take #{I_0} to be the full disjoint-union index set and
#{J_0} its retained subset, identifying their subtype functions
with the joint and retained coordinate spaces. It maps the joint
Gaussian to the Gaussian with restricted mean and covariance.}
\p{Equivalently, TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.Probability.map_affine_multivariateGaussian}
uses finite decidable source and target index types, arbitrary
rectangular array #{L}, translation #{c} and positive-semidefinite
covariance #{\widehat\Sigma}. Its Euclidean linear map is
#{U_ILU_{I\sqcup J_Y}^{-1}}. At mean zero and #{c=0}, its image
has mean zero and covariance #{L\widehat\Sigma L^T}.
Using the density identifications above and
#{LC_E=C_Xp_X} recovers #{(p_X)_*\mu_Q=\mu_S}.
These are identities between Gaussian probability measures;
they assert no volume preservation by projection. The raw factor
#{c_C} and absolute-integral identity remain supplied by the
intrinsic integral calculation of \ref{fdip-000E}.}
